export const BD6={title:'Building the affirmative case',hook:'The affirmative must convince judges that when there is not enough to go around, scarce resources should go to the patients most likely to live long lives afterward. Done poorly, this sounds cold. Done well, it sounds like responsible stewardship that saves the most lives and years of life. This lesson builds that case.',objectives:['Frame the affirmative case around stewardship and maximizing benefit.','Develop three strong affirmative contentions with evidence.','Define terms to set favorable but fair boundaries.','Anticipate negative attacks and prepare responses.'],sections:[
['Framing: stewardship, not worth',[
'Begin by framing the resolution as **stewardship** of scarce resources, not a judgment of anyone’s **worth**. A strong opening line: “Every patient has equal value. That is exactly why, when we cannot save everyone, we must use each scarce resource where it will save the most lives and the most years of life.” This framing defuses accusations of ageism or discrimination before the negative raises them.',
"Keep the framing consistent across all three affirmative speeches. If the second affirmative or summary speaker drifts into language that sounds like ranking people’s worth, the negative will seize on it.",
'Define key terms to make the resolution reasonable. For example, define “limited healthcare resources” as **truly scarce resources during shortages or crises** (ICU beds, ventilators, organs, drugs in shortage), and “greatest likelihood of long-term survival” as an **individualized, evidence-based assessment** by a triage team, not a stereotype based on age or disability.',
'Offer a clear **value** (for example, maximizing lives and life-years saved) and a **criterion** for judges to weigh the debate (for example, which side saves more lives with the same resources).',
order('Affirmative opening structure',['Hook: a vivid scarcity scenario','Resolution stated clearly','Definitions of key terms','Value and criterion','Preview of three contentions'])]],
['Contention 1: maximizing lives and life-years',[
'When resources are scarce, giving them to patients who are more likely to survive and live longer produces **more total lives and life-years saved**. This is the core of utilitarian ethics and of major allocation frameworks: Emanuel and colleagues recommended **maximizing benefits** as a primary value during COVID-19, and the AMA lists **likelihood and amount of benefit** as appropriate criteria.',
'Illustrate with numbers (labeled as hypothetical if invented): if 10 ventilators go to patients with an 80% chance of long-term survival, about 8 lives are saved; if they go to patients with a 20% chance, about 2 are saved. The difference represents real people who could have lived.',
'This contention also addresses **waste**: using scarce resources on patients very unlikely to survive can mean others die waiting.',
chk('Why is a hypothetical calculation useful in the affirmative case?',['It makes the cost of not maximizing benefit concrete—real lives lost','It replaces evidence','It proves the negative is wrong about everything','Judges require math'],'Label invented numbers as hypothetical.')]],
['Contention 2: established medical practice',[
'Survival-based allocation is **already part of medicine**. Kidney allocation uses **longevity matching** so that the longest-lasting kidneys go to the recipients expected to live longest; liver allocation considers who will benefit; crisis standards of care use **prognosis**; and during the 2023 chemotherapy shortage, guidance prioritized **curative-intent** treatment over palliative use when supplies ran short.',
'The affirmative can argue that the resolution simply asks medicine to apply this principle **consistently and transparently**, rather than relying on hidden, implicit rationing through waiting lists, insurance and geography that often harm vulnerable patients without accountability.',
'This contention gives judges confidence that the resolution is **practical**, not theoretical.',
match('Evidence and affirmative use',[['Kidney longevity matching','Survival already guides organ allocation'],['Crisis standards of care','Prognosis guides triage in emergencies'],['2023 chemotherapy shortage','Curative intent prioritized'],['AMA Code','Likelihood of benefit is an accepted criterion'],['Emanuel framework','Maximize benefits'],['Implicit rationing','Hidden rules are less fair than open ones']])]],
['Contention 3: fairness through transparency',[
'The affirmative can turn fairness into its strength. A **clear, published** survival-based rule, applied by a **triage committee** separate from the bedside clinician, is more **consistent** and **accountable** than ad hoc decisions or first come, first served, which favors people with better access to care. Accountability-for-reasonableness principles—publicity, relevance, appeals and enforcement—can all be met.',
'Address bias directly: the resolution supports **individualized** assessment, regular **reassessment**, and **excluding age and disability as categorical criteria**. Prognosis is about the patient’s likely response to treatment, not who they are.',
'A strong line: “The question is not whether we ration—we already do. The question is whether we ration in the open, using evidence, or in the shadows, using luck and privilege.”',
sort('Strong or weak affirmative argument?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Transparent rules applied by a triage committee',0],['“Older people have lived enough”',1],['Individualized, regularly reassessed prognosis',0],['Ignoring disability concerns',1],['First come, first served favors those with access',0]])]],
['Anticipating negative attacks',[
'Expect the negative to argue: **(1) discrimination** against older and disabled people; **(2) uncertain predictions**; **(3) prioritizing the worst off**; **(4) changing ideas of age** from longevity science; and **(5) civil rights guidance** against long-term survival criteria. Prepare a response to each.',
'Sample responses: (1) the resolution uses individualized clinical prognosis, not age or disability labels; (2) uncertainty exists in every medical decision, and evidence-based prediction is better than ignoring outcomes; (3) the worst off are also served when more lives overall are saved, and priority can still go to the worst off when outcomes are similar; (4) longevity science improves predictions, making individualized assessment more accurate; (5) guidance prohibits **stereotypes**, not **evidence-based** benefit assessments.',
'Keep responses respectful—judges score decorum and penalize dismissive tones.',
match('Negative attack and affirmative response',[['Discrimination','Individualized prognosis, not labels'],['Uncertain predictions','Evidence beats ignoring outcomes'],['Worst off deserve priority','More lives saved helps everyone; tie-breakers can favor the worst off'],['Longevity science','Improves individualized predictions'],['Civil rights guidance','Bars stereotypes, not evidence-based benefit'],['Coldness','Equal worth requires using resources well']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Affirmative opening',{a:{text:'You are the first affirmative speaker. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should come right after your hook?',choices:[['State the resolution, define terms fairly and preview three contentions','b','Correct.',true],['Attack the negative immediately','b','They have not spoken yet.',false]]},b:{text:'How should you define “greatest likelihood of long-term survival”?',choices:[['An individualized, evidence-based assessment by a triage team, not a judgment based on age or disability','c','Correct. Fair and defensible.',true],['Whoever is youngest','c','Too narrow and vulnerable to ageism attacks.',false]]},c:{text:'Your definitions make the negative’s discrimination argument harder to win.',end:true}})]],
["Affirmative evidence file",["Organize affirmative evidence into a printed **evidence file** with tabs for each contention and for each expected negative attack. For each card, include the claim, source and date, a short quotation or statistic and a one-line explanation of how to use it.","Useful affirmative cards include: Emanuel and colleagues’ recommendation to maximize benefits; the AMA’s list of appropriate criteria; OPTN longevity matching; crisis standards based on prognosis; curative-intent prioritization during drug shortages; and evidence that first come, first served disadvantages people with less access.","Practice finding any card within seconds. In a fast round, the team that can locate evidence quickly makes stronger, more specific arguments.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Tab in the affirmative file","buckets":["Contention evidence","Response to negative"],"items":[["Emanuel: maximize benefits",0],["Answer to the ageism argument",1],["OPTN longevity matching",0],["Answer to uncertain predictions",1],["2023 curative-intent prioritization",0],["Answer to civil rights guidance",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Frame the affirmative as stewardship—equal worth requires using scarce resources where they save the most lives and life-years; define resources as truly scarce and survival as individualized, evidence-based assessment.','Contentions: maximize lives and life-years (Emanuel, AMA likelihood of benefit), established practice (kidney longevity matching, crisis standards, 2023 curative-intent prioritization), and fairness through transparent rules and triage committees.','Prepare respectful responses to discrimination, uncertainty, worst-off priority, longevity science and civil rights attacks.'],terms:[['Stewardship','Responsible management of scarce resources.'],['Contention','A main argument in a debate case.'],['Criterion','A standard judges use to decide which side better meets the value.']],mcqs:[q('Which framing best protects the affirmative from ageism attacks?','Stewardship: equal worth requires using resources where they save the most lives','Older people matter less','Survival is all that matters','Ignore the issue','Equal worth.'),q('Which is established practice supporting the affirmative?','Kidney longevity matching','Vaccine priority for the highest-risk groups','First come, first served','Random lotteries for everything','Survival used in allocation.'),q('How should the affirmative define “long-term survival” assessment?','Individualized and evidence-based','Based on age alone','Based on disability status','Based on wealth','Fair definition.'),q('What is a value in a debate case?','The principle the case aims to uphold','A statistic','A rebuttal','A speaker’s name','Such as maximizing lives saved.'),q('Which affirmative argument turns fairness into a strength?','Transparent rules applied by a triage committee','Secret decisions','First come, first served','Ignoring bias','Accountability.'),q('Why label invented numbers as hypothetical?','To stay honest while illustrating impact','To confuse judges','Because judges require it','It is not needed','Credibility.'),q('How might the affirmative answer civil rights guidance?','It bars stereotypes, not evidence-based benefit assessments','Civil rights do not matter','Guidance is always wrong','Ignore it','Distinguish.'),q('What does implicit rationing often rely on?','Waiting lists, insurance and geography','Published policies','Triage committees','Lotteries','Hidden mechanisms.')],frq:{prompt:'Write an outline of the affirmative case for this year’s resolution, including framing, definitions, three contentions with evidence and responses to two likely negative attacks.',rubric:[['Framing and definitions',['stewardship','equal worth','define','scarce','individualized','evidence-based']],['Contentions',['maximize','lives','life-years','established','longevity matching','crisis standards','transparent']],['Evidence',['emanuel','ama','optn','chemotherapy','curative']],['Responses',['discrimination','uncertain','civil rights','worst off','respectful']]],model:'Framing: Every patient has equal worth, and that is why, when we cannot save everyone, we must use each scarce resource where it will save the most lives and years of life. Definitions: “limited healthcare resources” means resources that are truly scarce during shortages or crises, such as ICU beds, ventilators, donor organs and drugs in shortage; “greatest likelihood of long-term survival” means an individualized, evidence-based clinical assessment by a triage team, regularly reassessed, not a stereotype based on age or disability. Value: maximizing lives and life-years saved; criterion: which side saves more lives with the same resources. Contention 1: Maximizing benefit—giving scarce resources to patients most likely to survive long-term saves more lives and years, as Emanuel and colleagues recommended during COVID-19 and as the AMA’s criterion of likelihood of benefit supports. Contention 2: Established practice—kidney allocation already uses longevity matching, crisis standards of care rely on prognosis and during the 2023 chemotherapy shortage guidance prioritized curative-intent treatment. Contention 3: Fairness through transparency—published rules applied by triage committees are more accountable than hidden rationing through waiting lists and first come, first served, which favors those with better access. Responses: If the negative argues discrimination, we reply that the resolution uses individualized prognosis, not labels, and excludes categorical age or disability criteria. If they argue predictions are uncertain, we reply that uncertainty exists in all medicine and evidence-based estimates save more lives than ignoring outcomes.'},sources:[RULES,EMANUEL,OPTN,LANCET_FW]};

export const BD7={title:'Building the negative case: equity, ageism and changing ideas of age',hook:'Imagine being told you will not receive a ventilator because a formula predicts you will not live as many years as someone else—when that prediction was based on your age, a disability or an illness linked to where you grew up. The negative case argues that the resolution, however well-meant, turns predicted lifespan into a ticket for care. Your official references give the negative powerful evidence.',objectives:['Frame the negative case around equal worth, equity and humility about predictions.','Use Breaking the Age Code to argue that ageism distorts survival predictions.','Use longevity science to challenge chronological age as a predictor.','Develop negative contentions on disability, structural inequity and uncertainty.'],sections:[
['Framing: equal access, not predicted value',[
'Frame the negative around **equal moral worth** and **justice**: access to life-saving care should not depend on how many years a model predicts a person will live. The resolution makes **long-term** survival the deciding factor, which magnifies existing inequalities because life expectancy is shaped by age, disability, chronic illness, income, race and neighborhood.',
'Offer an alternative so judges do not think the negative opposes all rationing: for example, use **short-term benefit** to exclude only patients very unlikely to survive the immediate crisis, then use a **lottery** or other fair process among the rest, with priority for the worst off when appropriate. This mirrors many revised crisis standards.',
'Value: **justice** or **equal respect**. Criterion: which side better protects equal access for all patients, especially vulnerable groups.',
order('Negative opening structure',['Hook: a patient denied care by prediction','Restate the resolution and its flaw','Define long-term survival as predicted lifespan','Value and criterion','Preview contentions and alternative'])]],
['Contention: ageism and age beliefs',[
'Becca Levy’s **Breaking the Age Code** argues that **age beliefs**—the cultural stereotypes we absorb about aging—affect health. In a landmark study, Levy and colleagues found that older adults with **more positive self-perceptions of aging lived about 7.5 years longer** than those with negative views. Levy also describes cultural differences, such as Japanese women reporting fewer menopausal **hot flashes**, linked to more positive cultural views of aging.',
'The negative can argue that if beliefs and stereotypes influence health outcomes, then **survival predictions based on age reflect ageism** as much as biology—and allocation rules that rank people by predicted survival risk becoming **self-fulfilling prophecies**. WHO’s **Global Report on Ageism** (2021) found ageism is widespread in health care and harms older people’s health.',
'This contention lets the negative use the official reference directly, which judges and test questions reward.',
match('Levy evidence and negative use',[['Positive age beliefs linked to ~7.5 more years','Survival depends on more than biology'],['Japanese women and fewer hot flashes','Culture shapes how aging is experienced'],['Age stereotypes absorbed from culture','Predictions may embed ageism'],['WHO Global Report on Ageism','Ageism is widespread in health care'],['Self-fulfilling prophecy','Denying care confirms low predictions'],['Age beliefs can change','Outcomes are not fixed']])]],
['Contention: longevity science and biological age',[
'The GESDA report on longevity science notes that humanity has long marked age **chronologically**, but science is rapidly challenging that measure. Researchers now study **biological age**—measured with tools such as **epigenetic “aging clocks”**—and interventions that may slow aging are moving toward clinical use. Two people of the same chronological age may differ greatly in biological age and expected lifespan.',
'The negative can argue that **long-term survival predictions are moving targets**: treatments and science change survival rapidly, so today’s predictions may be wrong tomorrow. A criterion based on predicted lifespan could deny care to people whose outlook would have improved.',
'The affirmative may respond that better science improves individualized prediction; the negative should reply that improved tools for some do not fix bias or uncertainty for all, and that predictions years ahead remain highly uncertain at the bedside.',
flip('Longevity science terms',[['Chronological age','Years since birth'],['Biological age','How old the body’s systems appear'],['Epigenetic clock','A biomarker estimate of biological age'],['Geroscience','Studying aging as a driver of disease'],['Moving target','Survival predictions change as science advances'],['Negative use','Predicted lifespan is an unstable basis for allocation']])]],
['Contention: disability, chronic illness and structural inequity',[
'Long-term survival criteria tend to disadvantage people with **disabilities** and **chronic illnesses** whose life expectancy is shorter, even when they would benefit greatly from treatment now. During COVID-19, disability advocates challenged triage plans, and federal civil rights authorities worked with states to remove categorical exclusions and long-term life expectancy judgments.',
'**Structural inequities**—poverty, environmental exposures, unequal access to care and discrimination—lower life expectancy for some communities. A rule that favors those with the best long-term outlook can **reward existing advantage** and deepen health disparities. Tools such as SOFA have been criticized for potentially disadvantaging Black patients because of differences in baseline kidney function linked to structural factors.',
'This contention connects the debate to **justice** and **equity**, values many judges find compelling.',
sort('Supports negative contention on inequity?',['Supports','Does not support'],[['Life expectancy differs by neighborhood and income',0],['Civil rights complaints about COVID-19 triage',0],['Kidney longevity matching improves graft use',1],['SOFA concerns for Black patients',0],['Maximizing total life-years',1],['Chronic illness shortens predicted survival',0]])]],
['Contention: uncertainty and the worst off',[
'Predicting **long-term** survival at the bedside is far harder than predicting short-term survival. ICU scores were not designed for it, and confident-looking numbers can hide wide uncertainty. Errors cost lives: a patient denied a ventilator because of a pessimistic prediction cannot benefit if the prediction was wrong.',
'**Prioritarian** ethics argues that the **worst off**—often the sickest—deserve extra weight. The resolution may systematically deprioritize them. Even utilitarian frameworks like Emanuel’s used **lotteries** among patients with similar prognoses and did not rely on long-term survival alone.',
'A strong negative line: “The affirmative asks us to bet a person’s life on a prediction about years they have not yet lived.”',
bench('Is the negative claim supported by the invented evidence?',[{text:'Invented study summary: A long-term survival score correctly predicted five-year survival in 68% of ICU patients in one hospital.',claim:'The score was wrong for about one in three patients in that study.',supported:true,why:'If it was correct 68% of the time, it was wrong about 32% of the time.'},{text:'Invented study summary: A long-term survival score correctly predicted five-year survival in 68% of ICU patients in one hospital.',claim:'The score will be equally accurate in every hospital.',supported:false,why:'One hospital’s results may not generalize.'},{text:'Invented report: Average life expectancy in two neighborhoods of the same city differed by 12 years.',claim:'Long-term survival criteria could favor residents of the wealthier neighborhood.',supported:true,why:'If life expectancy reflects place, survival rules can reflect those gaps.'}])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Negative rebuttal',{a:{text:'The affirmative says, “We only use individualized prognosis, never age.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the strongest negative response?',choices:[['Individual long-term prognosis still reflects age, disability and structural inequity, so the effect is the same; and long-term predictions are uncertain','b','Correct.',true],['Agree and concede the round','b','Challenge the claim.',false]]},b:{text:'How can you use the official references?',choices:[['Cite Levy’s finding that age beliefs affect longevity and the GESDA report on changing measures of age','c','Correct.',true],['Avoid the references','c','Judges value reference-based evidence.',false]]},c:{text:'Your rebuttal ties the official references directly to the resolution’s weakness.',end:true}})]],
["Negative evidence file and alternative",["Build a printed negative file with tabs for each contention and each expected affirmative argument. Strong negative cards include Levy’s age-belief findings, WHO’s report on ageism, the GESDA longevity report, civil rights revisions to COVID-19 triage plans, concerns about SOFA and structural bias, and frameworks that use lotteries among similar patients.","Prepare a clear, one-sentence **alternative**: “Use short-term benefit to exclude only those very unlikely to survive the immediate crisis, then allocate fairly among the rest, giving priority to the worst off when outcomes are similar.” Repeat it in each negative speech so judges remember there is a fair option besides the resolution.","The negative wins most often by showing that the resolution’s harms to equity outweigh its benefits and that a fairer alternative still saves many lives.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Negative case flow","steps":["Frame equal worth and justice","Show how long-term survival embeds ageism","Show disability and structural inequity harms","Show uncertainty of long-term predictions","Offer the fair alternative","Weigh equity over predicted life-years"]}]]
],takeaways:['Frame the negative around equal worth and justice; offer an alternative (short-term benefit threshold, then lottery and priority for the worst off) so judges see you support fair rationing.','Use Levy’s Breaking the Age Code (positive age beliefs linked to about 7.5 more years; culture shapes aging) and WHO’s ageism report to argue survival predictions embed ageism; use GESDA longevity science to show biological age and predictions are moving targets.','Argue that long-term criteria disadvantage people with disabilities and chronic illness and reward structural advantage (civil rights complaints, SOFA concerns), and that long-term predictions are uncertain while the worst off deserve priority.'],terms:[['Ageism','Stereotyping and discrimination based on age.'],['Biological age','An estimate of how old the body’s systems are, which can differ from chronological age.'],['Self-fulfilling prophecy','A prediction that causes itself to come true.']],mcqs:[q('What symptoms do Japanese women experience less of during menopause due to the cultural perspective on aging and women?','Hot flashes','Weight gain','Irritability','Hair loss','HOSA sample question (Levy).'),q('Levy’s study found that positive self-perceptions of aging were linked to living about how many more years?','7.5','1','20','0','Longevity study.'),q('Biological age refers to:','How old the body’s systems appear','Years since birth','Age on a driver’s license','Retirement age','Can differ from chronological age.'),q('Which ethical view gives extra weight to the worst off?','Prioritarianism','Utilitarianism','Libertarianism','Egoism','Negative support.'),q('Which alternative might the negative propose?','A short-term benefit threshold followed by a lottery','No rationing at all','Only age-based priority','First come, first served only','Fair alternative.'),q('Why can long-term survival criteria deepen disparities?','Life expectancy reflects income, place and discrimination','They always favor the poor','They ignore all data','They use lotteries','Structural inequity.'),q('Which WHO report supports the negative’s ageism argument?','Global report on ageism','World malaria report','Global TB report','Vaccine safety report','2021.'),q('A prediction that causes itself to come true is a:','Self-fulfilling prophecy','Placebo','Lottery','Prognosis score','Denying care confirms low predictions.')],frq:{prompt:'Write an outline of the negative case, using at least two official references, and include an alternative approach to allocation.',rubric:[['Framing',['equal worth','justice','equity','predicted lifespan','value']],['References',['levy','breaking the age code','7.5','gesda','biological age','longevity']],['Contentions',['ageism','disability','chronic illness','structural','uncertain','worst off']],['Alternative',['short-term','threshold','lottery','priority','fair']]],model:'Framing: every person has equal worth, and access to life-saving care should not depend on how many years a model predicts someone will live; our value is justice and our criterion is which side better protects equal access, especially for vulnerable patients. Contention 1, ageism: In Breaking the Age Code, Becca Levy shows that age beliefs shape health—older adults with positive self-perceptions of aging lived about 7.5 years longer than those with negative views—and that culture changes how aging is experienced, such as Japanese women reporting fewer hot flashes. If stereotypes affect outcomes, survival predictions based on age reflect ageism as much as biology, and ranking patients by those predictions risks a self-fulfilling prophecy; WHO’s Global Report on Ageism confirms ageism is widespread in health care. Contention 2, changing measures of age: the GESDA longevity report explains that science is moving from chronological to biological measures of age and bringing longevity interventions toward the clinic, so long-term predictions are moving targets. Contention 3, disability and inequity: long-term criteria disadvantage people with disabilities, chronic illness and lower life expectancy caused by poverty or discrimination, which is why COVID-19 triage plans that used life expectancy drew civil rights complaints and were revised. Contention 4, uncertainty and the worst off: long-term survival is hard to predict at the bedside, and errors cost lives. Alternative: use short-term benefit only to exclude patients very unlikely to survive the immediate crisis, then use a lottery among the rest, with priority for the worst off when outcomes are similar.'},sources:[RULES,LEVY_2002,GESDA,WHO_AGEISM]};

export const BD8={title:'Debate format, roles and flowing',hook:'Six speeches, two minutes each, 30 seconds between them and no clock in your hand. The structure of Biomedical Debate rewards teams that know exactly what each speech must accomplish and who will give it. This lesson walks through the format, assigns roles and teaches the note-taking system debaters call flowing.',objectives:['Describe the order and purpose of each debate speech.','Assign team roles for affirmative and negative sides.','Use flowing to track arguments during the round.','Manage time without a timing device.'],sections:[
['The order of speeches',[
'Each match follows a fixed pattern: **(1) First Affirmative** (2 minutes) presents the affirmative arguments. **(2) First Negative** (2 minutes) responds to the affirmative’s arguments. **(3) Second Negative** (2 minutes) presents the negative’s own arguments. **(4) Second Affirmative** (2 minutes) responds to the negative’s arguments. **(5) Negative Summary/Rebuttal** (2 minutes) concludes for the negative. **(6) Affirmative Summary/Rebuttal** (2 minutes) concludes for the affirmative. **Thirty seconds** of transition time follows each speech for teams to confer and judges to score.',
'Notice that the **negative speaks twice in a row** (speeches 2 and 3) and the **affirmative gets the last word**. The first negative speaker must respond before presenting the negative’s own case, which comes in the second negative speech.',
'A team may begin a segment early, but it does not gain extra time, and the opponent still receives its full time.',
order('Speech order',['First Affirmative: present arguments','First Negative: respond to affirmative','Second Negative: present negative arguments','Second Affirmative: respond to negative','Negative Summary/Rebuttal','Affirmative Summary/Rebuttal'])]],
['What each speech must do',[
'**First Affirmative**: hook, resolution, definitions, value and criterion, and contentions with evidence—scored on persuasiveness, flow and logic and relevance. **First Negative**: directly answer the affirmative’s contentions with accurate, relevant **counterarguments**—a heavily weighted speech. **Second Negative**: present the negative’s own contentions and alternative with evidence, flow and relevance. **Second Affirmative**: answer the negative’s contentions with counterarguments and rebuild the affirmative case.',
'**Summary/rebuttal speeches** do not introduce brand-new arguments; they **clarify**, **compare** and **weigh** the clash, use **researched data** and explain why their side wins. Judges score evidence and effectiveness, clarification of argument and relevance.',
'Because response speeches are heavily weighted, teams must listen and adapt rather than read prepared scripts.',
match('Speech and main job',[['First Affirmative','Present the affirmative case'],['First Negative','Counter the affirmative case'],['Second Negative','Present the negative case'],['Second Affirmative','Counter the negative case'],['Negative Summary','Weigh the debate for the negative'],['Affirmative Summary','Final word, weigh the debate for the affirmative']])]],
['Assigning roles',[
'At least **three members must speak**. With three speakers, one approach: Speaker A gives the **first constructive** speech (1A or 2N), Speaker B gives the **response** speech (1N or 2A) and Speaker C gives the **summary/rebuttal**. A fourth member can act as **flow keeper and evidence finder**, passing notes and evidence cards, or share a speech by switching at the podium within the time limit (only one speaker at the podium at a time).',
'Assign roles by strengths: a clear, polished speaker for constructive speeches; a quick thinker for responses; a confident synthesizer for the summary. Prepare role assignments for **both sides** because the draw determines which speeches you give.',
'Team participation is scored—three members should speak with equal command of the topic.',
"Write each member’s role for both sides on an index card in your folder so there is no confusion after the draw.",
sort('Best fit for the role',['Constructive speaker','Response speaker','Summary speaker'],[['Polished delivery of prepared contentions',0],['Thinks quickly to counter new arguments',1],['Compares both cases and explains why your side wins',2],['Strong opening hook and definitions',0],['Listens closely and refutes point by point',1],['Calm closing under pressure',2]])]],
['Flowing: tracking the debate',[
'**Flowing** is structured note-taking. Divide paper into columns, one for each speech. Write each argument in shorthand in the column of the speech where it appears, then draw arrows to show responses. Use abbreviations (A1, A2 for affirmative contentions; N1, N2 for negative; “X” for dropped arguments).',
'Flowing reveals **dropped arguments**—points the other team never answered—which you can highlight in rebuttals: “The negative never responded to our evidence on kidney longevity matching.” It also prevents you from forgetting to answer an argument.',
"Use consistent symbols: a star for your strongest argument, a question mark for unclear claims to challenge and an arrow pointing back when an argument extends from an earlier speech. Practice flowing recorded debates or news interviews to build speed.",
'Assign one member to flow every speech while others prepare; pass notes quietly to maintain **table decorum**.',
order('Flowing a round',['Divide paper into six columns for the speeches','Write each argument in shorthand','Draw arrows to responses','Mark dropped arguments','Circle the most important clashes','Use the flow to plan rebuttals'])]],
['Managing time without a clock',[
'There are **no time warnings** during speeches or transitions, and **timing devices are not allowed**. The timekeeper calls time at the end, and speakers must **stop immediately**. Practice each speech with a timer until you can feel two minutes—roughly **250–300 spoken words**.',
'Build speeches with **flexible modules**: a core you always deliver and optional evidence you add or drop. Put your most important point **first**, so if you run short, the key idea is already made. End sections with short sentences you can stop on.',
'Use the **30-second transitions** wisely: confirm who speaks next, hand over the flow and pick the two or three most important responses.',
"If time is called mid-sentence, stop immediately and step back. Judges notice speakers who keep talking after time and may penalize delivery and decorum.",
chk('About how many spoken words fit in a two-minute speech?',['About 250–300','About 50','About 1,000','About 2,000'],'Practice to feel the time without a timer.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('After the first affirmative',{a:{text:'You are negative. The first affirmative presented three contentions. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must your first negative speech do?',choices:[['Answer the affirmative’s contentions with counterarguments','b','Correct. The negative case comes next.',true],['Present all your own contentions and ignore theirs','b','The first negative responds first.',false]]},b:{text:'Your flow shows the affirmative dropped your disability argument. When do you highlight it?',choices:[['In the negative summary/rebuttal','c','Correct.',true],['Never','c','Dropped arguments are powerful.',false]]},c:{text:'Your summary explains that the affirmative never answered a key argument.',end:true}})]],
["Preparing speech blocks",["Debaters prepare **blocks**—short, pre-written responses to arguments they expect. For each expected argument, write a 20–40 second block using the four-step refutation and one piece of evidence. Organize blocks by argument in your printed folder.","Blocks save time during transitions and help response speakers deliver accurate counterarguments. But do not read them mechanically: adapt each block to what the opponent actually said, using their words.","Build blocks for both sides. A team that has answers ready for the ten most likely arguments on each side will handle most rounds confidently.",{"t":"check","q":"What is a debate block?","options":["A short, pre-written response to an expected argument","A physical barrier","A penalty for going over time","A rule against speaking"],"why":"Prepare blocks for both sides."}]]
],takeaways:['Six 2-minute speeches with 30-second transitions: 1A presents, 1N counters, 2N presents, 2A counters, negative summary, affirmative summary (last word); starting early gives no extra time.','Constructive speeches are scored on persuasiveness, flow and relevance; response speeches on counterarguments; summaries on evidence, clarification and relevance without brand-new arguments.','At least three members speak; assign constructive, response and summary roles for both sides; flow every speech to catch dropped arguments; no timers—practice about 250–300 words per two minutes and lead with your key point.'],terms:[['Flowing','Structured note-taking that tracks arguments across a debate.'],['Dropped argument','An argument the other side never answered.'],['Constructive speech','A speech that presents a side’s main arguments.']],mcqs:[q('Which speech comes first?','First Affirmative','First Negative','Negative Summary','Second Affirmative','Then First Negative.'),q('How long is each speech?','2 minutes','5 minutes','30 seconds','10 minutes','With 30-second transitions.'),q('Which side gets the final speech?','Affirmative','Negative','Neither','The judges','Affirmative Summary.'),q('What does the First Negative do?','Responds to the affirmative’s arguments','Gives the final summary','Presents only new negative contentions','Times the round','Counterarguments.'),q('Are timing devices allowed?','No','Yes','Only phones','Only for the captain','Rely on the timekeeper.'),q('What is a dropped argument?','An argument the other side never answered','A false argument','A quoted statistic','A transition','Highlight it.'),q('How many team members must speak?','At least three','One','Two','Five','Team participation.'),q('Should summary speeches introduce brand-new arguments?','No—they clarify and weigh','Yes, always','Only statistics','Only jokes','Weigh the clash.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Biomedical Debate speech order and describe how your team would assign roles and use flowing to succeed in a round.',rubric:[['Speech order',['first affirmative','first negative','second negative','second affirmative','summary','30 seconds']],['Speech purposes',['present','respond','counterarguments','rebuttal','weigh','no new']],['Roles',['three','constructive','response','summary','fourth','both sides']],['Flowing and time',['flow','columns','dropped','notes','decorum','no timer','practice']]],model:'Each match has six two-minute speeches with 30 seconds of transition after each. The First Affirmative presents the affirmative case; the First Negative responds to it; the Second Negative presents the negative’s own case; the Second Affirmative responds to the negative and rebuilds; the Negative Summary concludes for the negative; and the Affirmative Summary gives the final word. Constructive speeches are judged on persuasiveness, flow and relevance, response speeches heavily on counterarguments, and summaries on evidence, clarification and relevance without new arguments. Because sides are drawn when we enter, we assign roles for both. Our clearest speaker, Maya, gives the constructive speech—1A or 2N; our quickest thinker, Leo, gives the response speech—1N or 2A; and our strongest closer, Priya, gives the summary. Our fourth member, Sam, flows every speech and organizes evidence cards, passing notes quietly to keep table decorum, and Sam can share a speech at the podium if needed. Flowing means dividing paper into six columns, writing each argument in shorthand and drawing arrows to responses, so we never forget to answer a point and can spot arguments the other team dropped, which Priya highlights in the summary. Since timing devices are not allowed and no warnings are given, we practice each speech to about 250–300 words, put the key point first and use transitions to choose our top responses.'},sources:[RULES,OWL_ARG]};

export const BD9={title:'Constructing speeches, evidence and rebuttals',hook:'Judges reward arguments that are persuasive, logical, relevant and supported—and counterarguments that are accurate and strong. Two minutes leaves no room for rambling. This lesson gives you structures for building arguments, using evidence, refuting the other side and spotting weak reasoning.',objectives:['Structure arguments with claim, warrant, evidence and impact.','Use evidence accurately and cite it orally.','Refute arguments with the four-step method.','Identify logical fallacies in your opponents’ and your own arguments.'],sections:[
['Claim, warrant, evidence, impact',[
'A strong argument has four parts. The **claim** is what you assert (“Survival-based allocation saves more lives”). The **warrant** explains **why** it is true (“because resources go to patients most likely to benefit”). The **evidence** supports it (a study, policy or expert framework). The **impact** explains **why it matters** (“that means more families keep their loved ones”).',
'Many debaters state claims and evidence but forget the **warrant** or **impact**. Judges score **flow and logic**—arguments that clearly connect reasons to conclusions—and **persuasiveness**, which often depends on impact.',
'Practice turning each piece of evidence into a full argument with all four parts.',
"**Signposting** helps judges follow your logic. Number your points (“First… second… third…”) and name the argument you are answering (“On their second contention about transparency…”). Signposting improves your flow and logic score and makes the judges’ notes match yours.",
order('Building an argument',['Claim','Warrant (why it is true)','Evidence (support)','Impact (why it matters)'])]],
['Using evidence well',[
'Cite evidence **orally** with the source and date: “According to Emanuel and colleagues in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020…” or “Becca Levy’s research found…”. Use **specific** facts rather than vague claims, and explain how the evidence supports your point.',
'Organize printed evidence in folders by **argument** and **side**, with tabs. Quote briefly; paraphrase longer material. Never **fabricate** or exaggerate evidence—judges score accuracy, and opponents may challenge a source.',
'Prefer recent, credible sources: peer-reviewed research, professional codes, government guidance and the official references. Note when a source is a commentary or opinion rather than research.',
sort('Strong or weak evidence use?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“Emanuel et al. (2020) recommended maximizing benefits”',0],['“Some guy online said…”',1],['Citing Levy’s longevity study with its finding',0],['An invented statistic presented as fact',1],['A recent professional ethics code',0],['“Everyone knows…”',1]])]],
['The four-step refutation',[
'Use a simple structure to answer opponents: **(1) “They say…”**—restate the argument fairly and briefly. **(2) “But…”**—state your response. **(3) “Because…”**—give your reasoning and evidence. **(4) “Therefore…”**—explain the impact on the round. For example: “They say survival rules are transparent. But transparency does not make a biased rule fair, because long-term life expectancy reflects age, disability and income. Therefore, a transparent rule would openly deepen inequity.”',
'Prioritize: answer the **most important** arguments first, group similar arguments together and point out **dropped** arguments. Do not try to answer everything weakly; answer key points strongly.',
'Counterarguments are worth significant points in the first negative and second affirmative speeches, so practice this structure until it is automatic.',
"Short, sharp refutations are better than long ones. In a two-minute response speech, aim to answer three or four arguments in about 30 seconds each, leaving a few seconds to summarize why your side is ahead.",
order('Four-step refutation',['They say: restate their argument','But: your response','Because: reasoning and evidence','Therefore: impact on the round'])]],
['Weighing and summary speeches',[
'Summary/rebuttal speeches must **clarify** the debate and explain **why your side wins**. Identify the **two or three key clashes**, explain where your side won each one and **weigh** impacts—compare **magnitude** (how big), **probability** (how likely), **timeframe** (how soon) and **who is affected**.',
'Example weighing: “Even if the affirmative saves some additional life-years, our impact is larger in scope: a rule that systematically disadvantages older and disabled patients harms millions and erodes trust in medicine.” Or for the affirmative: “Even if predictions are imperfect, ignoring prognosis means more preventable deaths every time resources run short.”',
'End with a concise final line restating your side’s value and why judges should vote for you.',
match('Weighing factor and question',[['Magnitude','How big is the impact?'],['Probability','How likely is it to happen?'],['Timeframe','How soon will it happen?'],['Scope','How many people are affected?'],['Reversibility','Can the harm be undone?'],['Value link','Which side better upholds the value?']])]],
['Logical fallacies to avoid and expose',[
'**Fallacies** are errors in reasoning. **Straw man**: misrepresenting the opponent’s argument to attack a weaker version (“The affirmative wants to abandon older people”). **Slippery slope**: claiming one step will inevitably lead to extreme results without evidence. **Ad hominem**: attacking people rather than arguments. **False dilemma**: presenting only two options when more exist (“either survival rules or chaos”). **Appeal to emotion** without evidence. **Hasty generalization** from a single case.',
'Point out fallacies **respectfully**: “That argument misrepresents our position; we never proposed age as a criterion.” Avoid fallacies yourself—judges score accuracy and relevance.',
'Knowing fallacies also helps on the written test and in everyday reasoning about health claims.',
"A powerful advanced move is the **turn**: showing that the opponent’s argument actually supports your side. For example, if the affirmative argues transparency is important, the negative can turn it: “Transparency would reveal exactly how this rule disadvantages older and disabled patients, which is why the public would reject it.”",
match('Fallacy and example',[['Straw man','“They want to abandon older people”'],['Slippery slope','“Next they will ration all care by age”'],['Ad hominem','Attacking the speaker, not the argument'],['False dilemma','“Either survival rules or chaos”'],['Hasty generalization','One case proves every case'],['Appeal to emotion','Feelings without evidence']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Refuting a claim',{a:{text:'The affirmative says, “Without survival rules, triage becomes chaos.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What fallacy is this?',choices:[['False dilemma—other fair methods exist, such as short-term thresholds and lotteries','b','Correct.',true],['Ad hominem','b','It does not attack a person.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you structure your response?',choices:[['They say… But… Because… Therefore…','c','Correct. Four-step refutation.',true],['Just say “That’s wrong”','c','Explain your reasoning.',false]]},c:{text:'Your refutation names the alternative and weighs why it is fairer.',end:true}})]],
["Clash and extension",["**Clash** is direct engagement between opposing arguments. Judges reward debaters who respond to what the other side actually said instead of repeating their own case. In each response speech, address the opponent’s arguments in the order they were presented, or group them by theme.","**Extending** an argument means carrying it forward into later speeches, showing it still stands after the other side’s attacks. Say, “Extend our first contention: they never answered the evidence on longevity matching, so it stands.” Arguments that are not extended may be treated as dropped by your own team.","Good clash and extension create a clear story: our arguments survived, theirs did not, and here is why our side wins.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Debate term and meaning","pairs":[["Clash","Direct engagement between opposing arguments"],["Extend","Carry an argument into later speeches"],["Drop","Fail to answer an argument"],["Turn","Show the opponent’s argument actually supports your side"],["Weigh","Compare the importance of impacts"],["Signpost","Tell judges which argument you are addressing"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Build arguments with claim, warrant, evidence and impact; cite evidence orally with source and date; organize printed folders by argument and side; never fabricate evidence.','Refute with “They say, But, Because, Therefore,” answering the most important arguments first and pointing out dropped arguments.','In summaries, identify key clashes and weigh magnitude, probability, timeframe and scope; expose fallacies (straw man, slippery slope, ad hominem, false dilemma, hasty generalization) respectfully and avoid them yourself.'],terms:[['Warrant','The reasoning that connects evidence to a claim.'],['Refutation','Answering and weakening an opponent’s argument.'],['Straw man','Misrepresenting an argument to make it easier to attack.']],mcqs:[q('Which part of an argument explains why a claim is true?','Warrant','Impact','Hook','Citation','Reasoning link.'),q('What is the first step of the four-step refutation?','They say…','Therefore…','But…','Because…','Restate fairly.'),q('Which weighing factor asks how many people are affected?','Scope','Timeframe','Probability','Magnitude','Breadth.'),q('“Either survival rules or chaos” is an example of:','False dilemma','Ad hominem','Straw man','Hasty generalization','Only two options.'),q('Attacking the speaker instead of the argument is:','Ad hominem','Slippery slope','False dilemma','Appeal to evidence','Personal attack.'),q('How should evidence be cited in a speech?','Orally with source and date','Not at all','Only by page number','With a slide','Credibility.'),q('What should summary speeches do?','Clarify key clashes and weigh impacts','Introduce brand-new contentions','Repeat the first speech word for word','Thank the judges only','Weigh.'),q('Misrepresenting an opponent’s argument is a:','Straw man','Warrant','Value','Criterion','Fallacy.')],frq:{prompt:'Using the four-step refutation, write a response to one argument the other side is likely to make on this year’s resolution, and explain how you would weigh the clash in a summary speech.',rubric:[['Restate',['they say','argument','fairly']],['Response and reasoning',['but','because','evidence','source','levy','emanuel','longevity matching']],['Impact',['therefore','impact','round','lives','equity']],['Weighing',['magnitude','probability','timeframe','scope','weigh','value']]],model:'As the negative, I would respond to the affirmative’s claim that survival-based allocation is transparent and therefore fair. They say: “A published survival rule applied by a triage committee is more transparent and accountable than hidden rationing.” But transparency does not make an unfair criterion fair. Because long-term life expectancy reflects age, disability, chronic illness and income, a survival rule openly ranks people by factors largely outside their control—and Becca Levy’s research showing that positive age beliefs are linked to about 7.5 more years of life suggests that age-based predictions reflect stereotypes as much as biology. Therefore, a transparent survival rule would simply make inequity official, and the affirmative’s transparency benefit does not outweigh the injustice. In the negative summary, I would identify the key clash as whether maximizing predicted life-years outweighs equal access. I would weigh magnitude and scope: even if the affirmative saves some additional life-years in a shortage, the resolution would systematically disadvantage millions of older and disabled patients every time resources are scarce. On probability, I would note that long-term predictions are uncertain, so the affirmative’s claimed benefits are less likely than they appear, while the harm to excluded patients is certain. I would also note that the affirmative never answered our alternative of a short-term threshold followed by a lottery, then close by restating our value of justice.'},sources:[RULES,OWL_ARG,LEVY_2002]};

export const BD10={title:'Delivery, decorum and the qualifying test',hook:'Two teams can make the same arguments; the one that sounds confident, respectful and organized usually wins. And none of it matters if your team does not qualify through the Round One test. This final lesson covers voice, stage presence, decorum and team participation—and a study plan for the test that seeds the bracket.',objectives:['Apply the overall debate quality criteria: voice, stage presence, diction, decorum and participation.','Maintain professional table decorum and respectful clash.','Prepare efficiently for the 50-question qualifying test.','Practice through full simulated rounds.'],sections:[
['Voice, stage presence and diction',[
'Each side is scored on **voice** (loud enough, varied rate and volume, appropriate pauses), **stage presence** (purposeful gestures, comfortable posture, eye contact, enthusiasm) and **diction, pronunciation and grammar** (clear enunciation, no fillers such as “um” and “like,” tone that fits the message).',
'In debate, speak at a **measured pace**—fast enough to make your points, slow enough for judges to follow. Emphasize key phrases, such as your value and contentions, and pause before important evidence. Look at the **judges**, not the opponents, when speaking.',
'Pronounce terms correctly: **prioritarianism**, **utilitarianism**, **epigenetic**, **comorbidity**. Practice difficult words until they flow naturally.',
"Record practice speeches on video and review them as a team. Notice posture, gestures and filler words, and agree on one improvement for each speaker before the next practice.",
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Eye contact with the judges',0],['Reading every word from notes',1],['Pausing before key evidence',0],['Constant “um” and “like”',1],['Varied volume for emphasis',0],['Speaking so fast judges cannot follow',1]])]],
['Decorum and respectful clash',[
'**Decorum** is professional behavior toward the other team. All statements must be **respectful and appropriate**; do not **interrupt**, talk over others or mock opponents. Attack **arguments**, not people: say “That argument overlooks…” rather than “They clearly don’t understand.”',
'**Table decorum** is evaluated throughout. You may write notes and whisper with teammates during the debate, but do so quietly without distracting the other team. Avoid eye-rolling, laughing or loud reactions. Shake hands and thank the other team afterward.',
'Respectful clash is persuasive: judges trust debaters who stay calm and courteous under pressure, especially on a sensitive topic about life and death.',
"Remember that real patients and families live with these decisions. Speak about older adults, people with disabilities and clinicians with dignity, whichever side you argue. Sensitivity strengthens credibility.",
sort('Professional decorum?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Quietly passing notes to a teammate',0],['Laughing while the opponent speaks',1],['“That argument overlooks the evidence on disability”',0],['“They obviously didn’t do any research”',1],['Thanking the other team after the round',0],['Interrupting the opposing speaker',1]])]],
['Team participation',[
'Judges award top **team participation** scores when there is **shared collaboration** and **three members speak** with **equal knowledge** of the topic. A team where one person dominates scores lower, even if that person is excellent.',
'Make sure every speaker can explain the core ideas of both cases, not just their own speech. During transitions, confer efficiently and support each other. If a teammate stumbles, pass a helpful note rather than taking over.',
'In practice, rotate roles so everyone can give constructive, response and summary speeches—useful when sides are drawn and if a teammate is ill.',
"Before competition, agree on simple signals for the table—such as tapping a card to request evidence—so teammates can communicate without whispering loudly. Small habits like these keep the team coordinated and protect your decorum score.",
chk('Which team earns the highest team participation score?',['Three members speak, each showing equal knowledge','One expert speaks the whole time','Members argue with each other at the table','Only two members speak'],'Shared collaboration is rewarded.')]],
['Preparing for the qualifying test',[
'The **Round One test** has **50 multiple-choice questions in 60 minutes**, all from the five official references. The **team average** determines qualification and seeding, so **every member** must study. There are no time announcements at ILC; watch the clock yourself.',
'Divide the references for summarizing, then **share** summaries so everyone studies everything. Focus on **definitions** (micro, meso, macro; prioritarianism; QALY), **frameworks** and **principles**, **specific examples** (WHO 3 by 5, COVID-19 allocation) and **research findings** (Levy’s age-belief studies). Note page numbers; sample questions cite them.',
'Create practice questions from each chapter and quiz each other weekly. Review the sample questions in the guidelines carefully.',
"On test day, read every option before choosing, eliminate clearly wrong answers first and mark difficult questions to revisit. Answer every question, since unanswered questions earn no points.",
order('Test study plan',['Read all five references','Divide summaries among members','Share and study every summary','Write practice questions by chapter','Quiz each other weekly','Take a timed 50-question practice test'])]],
['Simulated rounds',[
'The best preparation is **full simulated rounds**: draw sides at random, run six timed speeches with 30-second transitions, flow every speech and have coaches or classmates score with the official rating sheet. Debate against other teams when possible to face unfamiliar arguments.',
'After each round, debrief: Which arguments were strongest? What was dropped? Where did timing go wrong? How was decorum and delivery? Update evidence folders and rebuttal blocks with what you learned.',
'Aim to complete many practice rounds on **both sides** before competition, so the draw never surprises you.',
order('Simulated round routine',['Draw sides at random','Run six timed speeches with transitions','Flow every speech','Score with the rating sheet','Debrief strengths and gaps','Update evidence and rebuttal blocks'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('A heated moment',{a:{text:'Your opponent says your argument is “ridiculous.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you respond?',choices:[['Stay calm and respond to the argument with evidence','b','Correct. Decorum is scored.',true],['Call their argument ridiculous too','b','Unprofessional responses lose points.',false]]},b:{text:'One teammate wants to give all three speeches. What do you advise?',choices:[['Share speeches so at least three members speak equally','c','Correct.',true],['Let the best speaker do everything','c','Team participation is scored.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team stays professional and balanced, earning strong overall quality scores.',end:true}})]],
["Handling nerves and the bracket day",["Bracket day can be long: teams wait in a **holding room** until called and must remain there between rounds, without communicating with other teams. Bring water and a snack, keep evidence folders organized and use waiting time to review blocks quietly.","Before each round, take slow breaths and confirm roles for both sides. If a round goes badly, debrief briefly and refocus—each match is a fresh start. Teams more than **five minutes late** forfeit, so stay close and listen for your number.","Confidence comes from preparation. A team that has practiced many rounds on both sides will recognize most arguments and respond calmly.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Bracket day routine","steps":["Arrive early in business attire","Check in and wait in the holding room","Review blocks quietly","Confirm roles for both sides","Debate, then return to the holding room","Debrief briefly and refocus"]},"Remember that there are no observers, so parents and advisors cannot watch; support each other as a team."]]
],takeaways:['Voice, stage presence and diction: measured pace, varied volume, pauses, eye contact with judges, no fillers and correct pronunciation of technical terms.','Decorum is scored: respect, no interrupting or mocking, quiet note-passing and attacking arguments, not people; team participation rewards three members speaking with equal knowledge.','The 50-question qualifying test draws from the five references and uses the team average—everyone studies everything; prepare with practice questions and full simulated rounds on both sides.'],terms:[['Decorum','Professional, respectful behavior toward the other team.'],['Team participation','Shared collaboration with members speaking equally.'],['Simulated round','A full practice debate under competition conditions.']],mcqs:[q('Which shows good decorum?','Quietly passing notes to a teammate','Interrupting the opponent','Laughing at the other team','Rolling your eyes','Professional behavior.'),q('Where should speakers look while speaking?','At the judges','At the floor','Only at notes','At the opponents','Eye contact.'),q('How is the Round One test used?','Team average qualifies and seeds teams','It counts for half the final score','It chooses sides','It is optional','Qualifying.'),q('How many questions are on the test?','50','100','25','200','60 minutes.'),q('Which earns the best team participation score?','Three members speaking with equal knowledge','One member dominating','Two members only','No one speaking','Shared collaboration.'),q('Are time announcements given during ILC testing?','No','Yes, every 10 minutes','Only at the end','Only on request','Monitor your own time.'),q('What should you do after a simulated round?','Debrief and update evidence and rebuttals','Forget it','Change topics','Stop practicing','Improve.'),q('Which is a vocal filler?','“Um”','A pause','Evidence','A citation','Avoid fillers.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team will prepare for both the qualifying test and the debate rounds, including delivery, decorum and team participation.',rubric:[['Test prep',['50','references','team average','summaries','practice questions','page']],['Delivery',['voice','pace','pauses','eye contact','fillers','pronunciation']],['Decorum and participation',['respect','interrupt','notes','three','equal','collaboration']],['Simulated rounds',['draw sides','timed','flow','rating sheet','debrief','both sides']]],model:'For the qualifying test, every member will read all five official references, because the team average decides qualification and seeding. We will divide chapters for detailed summaries but share them so everyone studies everything, focusing on definitions such as micro-, meso- and macro-level allocation and prioritarianism, frameworks for allocating scarce resources, examples like WHO’s 3 by 5 initiative and Becca Levy’s research on age beliefs, noting page numbers. Each week we will write practice questions from our chapters, quiz each other and take timed 50-question practice tests, watching the clock because no time announcements are given. For the debate, we will run full simulated rounds: drawing sides at random, giving six timed two-minute speeches with 30-second transitions, flowing every speech and scoring ourselves with the official rating sheet, then debriefing and updating our evidence folders and rebuttal blocks. For delivery, we will practice a measured pace, varied volume, pauses before key evidence, eye contact with the judges and correct pronunciation of terms like prioritarianism and epigenetic, recording ourselves to remove fillers. For decorum, we will attack arguments, never people, avoid interrupting or reacting visibly and pass notes quietly at the table. For team participation, we will rotate roles so at least three members can give any speech with equal command of both cases.'},sources:[RULES,EMANUEL,LEVY_2002]};
